t of Czechoslovakia and so  therefore they went to Czech school and  they spoke both German and Czech in 39  they escaped to Sweden where I was born  in 1945 and at the age of 5 we moved  when I was five we moved to Canada I  have no recollection of transitioning  from Swedish to English at home we  always my brother and I spoke English  with my parents they told us we're in  Canada now you will speak English  and we my brother and I my parents spoke  German to each other we resented German  we resented hearing it  we resented everything having to do with  Europe because my parents would always  say that Canadians were lazy Canada had  no culture and so forth and everything  European was good and they wanted us to  be make friends with the children of  their European friends and those were  the last people we wanted to make  friends with so there was this  resistance to German now things changed  when I was 19 after two years at McGill  University and I was working in a summer  job on construction and then I was laid  off and I thought you know what I think  I'll go off to Europe so I went down to  the Port of Montreal and spent three  days trying to see the captains of  different ships in the port on the third  day I found a captain of a German ship  who had lost a sailor in Quebec City and  said yeah you can work your way across  with us so for ten days I was on this  small [ __ ] steamer which was just up  and down off across the Atlantic I was  hammering away at rust and scraping and  painting uh and that was the first time  that it actually meant something to me  to try and say something in German and I  had my little German book with the you  know the declensions of the mounds and  the pronouns and it was just horrendous  but at least then German became  meaningful to me briefly and then I  hitchhiked around Europe including in  Germany I can remember even  on a usually state in youth hostels I  arrived in I think it was burbling in  and it was pouring with rain after 11  o'clock at night and I went to their  local jail and asked if I could stay  there and they accommodated me very  comfortable jail um  and then I also worked two weeks on  construction in Vienna where I spoke  some German although most of the other  workers were Turks and that was it and  then I studied for three years in France  did nothing with German until 1987 when  I was between jobs you know I was after  my 3 years in France I joined the  Canadian Diplomatic Service was sent to  Hong Kong to learn Mandarin Chinese  subsequently went to Japan then I left  the embassy went to work for a major  Canadian forest products exporting  company and so that's through the 70s  and then in 1987 I left I was between  jobs and I decided to learn German I'm  not quite sure why so I went to all of  the secondhand bookstores in Vancouver  and I found lots of books textbooks that  other people had used you know things  like this and they're full of texts more  or less boring texts and vocabulary  which is what I needed they also have  you boolean drills and stuff which I  have always resisted doing in language  learning I don't like them I if anything  all I do with them is I look up the  answers so I can see some concentrated  examples of whatever pattern they're  trying to test you on but I feel it's  not that useful to to try and scratch  your brain to answer these questions  at any rate I didn't bring them all down  I showed more of them in my German  language video there were lots of them  and of course interesting about German  history culture and so forth and of  course even though I initially was kind  of resisting German the more you get  into a language the more you find it  interesting and this is another I think  important point people say I'm not that  interested in whatever get into it start  into it you may find that as they say in  French La Petite via on mahjong in other  words the appetite grows with the eating  and so then you get more and more into  it you'll find it in  thing as soon as possible I tried to get  away from these sorry lab textbooks one  very good source was a series of audio  cassettes of ordinary people talking and  I listened to those interviews many many  times I can remember them one was an  interview with a locomotive engineer who  had you know driven the train when the  Queen of England was on the train and  another guy was talking about how  farmers in Bavaria can't find wives  because they smell a whole bunch of  stuff but real people and I've always  felt that listening to real people  speaking if it's transcribed and you get  a chance to I didn't have transcripts  but ideally transcripts is an ideal way  to learn and that's why when we set up  link I began and initially they only  offered English and I started  interviewing lots of people and creating  content on link with transcripts because  I felt that if you give people the  chance to feel that they're  eavesdropping on a genuine conversation  that that's very effective learning  material so that was more or less what I  did with German stayed with it then it  so happened that I set up my own company  in the trade of wood products and we  were in the early 90s selling wood to  Germany so I was now travelling to  Germany and so then I tried even harder  to learn German bought books read and of  course spoke with people and gradually  my German improved and I also began to  appreciate German culture more and you  know just to digress in my previous  videos about Russian or Spanish you know  I explained or French some of the  attractiveness of those cultures and  German culture is often seen as you know  less fun than let's say Italian or  Spanish you know the Sun the music and  so forth but you have to appreciate  cultures for what they are and I think  the thing about German culture is a  sense of comfort solid comfort you stay  in a German or  tell I mean the doors closed solidly the  windows are solid and and so German  culture to me is is less about you know  dancing and singing it's more about you  know it's like you're sitting there  reading a book or one of these very  serious German newspapers and Bach is  playing in the background and and it's  raining outside and but there's that's  comfortable too  you know I like that and I like the  there their pubs with the wonderful  wooden paneling and and and so you kind  of learn to appreciate each culture for  what it is and of course the the towns  in Germany especially the smaller towns  are absolutely beautiful and Germany  into historically you know shallow man  who's you know revered in France was in  fact the German a franc Germans have had  a major imprint on European history  Martin Luther of course the 30 Years War  by the way there's an excellent audio  book on the 30 Years War which I've  listened to many times ah and of course  in the more recent past there's been you  know with two world wars where we won't  go into that subject but today in  Germany and I can say this as someone of  a Jewish background that no country has  addressed you know their own recent  history the way the Germans have it's  amazing it's amazing to walk around  Hamburg and see plaques with the names  of Jewish people who used to live in  that house and so I mean as far as I'm  concerned you know as my uncle once said  the Germans of that generation of the  generation of the war he doesn't want to  have anything to do with but Germans of  subsequent generation absolutely no  problem at all and in fact the Germans I  find are very friendly I recently drove  through Germany people and you did to  the you know the superficial contact  that I have with them in hotels or gas  stations whatever super friendly and  there's a hundred million German  speakers in Europe so it's an extremely  important language so I think there's so  many positive reasons now some people  say they like German rock music I don't  do rock music hard rock all  stuff right whatever I don't know you  know what it is I have no interest but  in fact I'm a bit old-fashioned I like  good you know so I like listening to  audiobooks of good I love his language  and you can get the texts on the  internet and so I can import them in the  link and study them you know what else I  like reading about history so you know  for something like this for example and  unfortunately many of these books you  know they're they're are you probably  see it there's a lot of words that I  don't know I would prefer to read these  on link so I could look them up and  they're in the words but when you're  down to not too many words that you  don't know then it's not that big a  chore to read without knowing all the  words I'm trying to think of what else I  wanted to say about German yeah I mean  it's an extremely important language I  think a lot of people in Eastern Europe  now are because of the economic might of  Germany Germany is German in Eastern  Europe in Russia is becoming the next  important language after English and  it's it's a fascinating in terms of its  literature in terms of history in terms  of philosophy it is a fascinating  culture I am NOT into science or  medicine but even their course German  has in the past been very important I  wanted to finish off with with one other  point that has come up and that's this  issue of talent you know some people say  to me oh you have a talent for languages  or I have like talking about themselves  I have no talent for languages  I don't think talent is all that  important granted you may sit there and  say well Steve you know your parents  spoke German to each other you heard  German at home you spoke Swedish for the  first five years of your life therefore  you can learn other languages more  easily I can't do that say some people  but then I look around and I see all of  the wonderful polyglots that I know in  our polyglot community on the Internet  if I think of Luca who grew up  unilingual Italian Richard simcha grew  up unilingual English if I think of all  the Swedes that I know or the Dutchman  who are Germans who are so fluent in  English  I it's not about Talent some people may  be better than others in terms of their  ability to imitate accent but  overwhelmingly it's your motivation your  interest in the language your sense that  you will achieve your goal that you're  willing to stay with it spend the time  and of course to develop that ability to  notice and that's so important and  that's why I made reference at the  beginning of my talk about how taking a  break from German Spanish Italian  actually improved my ability to notice  what's going on in German Spanish in  Italian and that's why for example when  I read away from Linc I will always  underline words and phrases and of  course in link that those of you aren't  familiar with Linc you save words and  phrases which we call creating links  well I create them by the tens of  thousand the more you can create those  links and then they reappear highlighted  in yellow that draws your attention to  words to phrases to patterns and you  have to do that in order to help the  brain notice otherwise we notice some  things but we don't notice other things  and the more things we can notice that  the brain has already grown accustomed  to noticing the more new things we can  notice so to finish off then I think  rather I would like to say rather than  focusing on talent worrying about  whether you have talent or not focusing  focus on developing the ability to  notice so there you have it again I ran  over my ten minutes but some complained  that my videos were too long and others  said they didn't mind
 

